#******************************************************
#......... LISTENER POSITION ........
#******************************************************

listener_x_y_coordinate_positions_file=/tmp/listenerCoordinates
#
# NOTE: To simulate source sound movement between speakers--as in 
# conventional stereo or surround sound setups--the listener position 
# should be no closer to either of the adjacent speakers between which movement 
# is to be simulated than the speakers are to each other. In other words,
# for convincing movement panning, the listener should be no closer than 
# the classic X/Y recording stereo listening sweet spot, often imagined 
# as forming an equilateral or isosceles triangle with the speakers. 
# Closer positions will cause the amplitude of sources positioned in between
# to increasingly dip and therefore destroy the illusion of a moving source sound. 

#******************************************************
#......... SOURCE POSITION TRAJECTORY ........
#******************************************************
# The sequence of source positions is created using values
# from the normalized movement function, iterated equidistantly across its
# range, to access the movement path across its range, 
# normalized to the movement function's 0 to 1 range.
# 
# The path is specified using a Cartesian origin, angle, and radius. 
# All three can be controlled using functions, which allows users to 
# specify paths in either Cartesian or polar coordinates, or a combination of both.
#
# (a) For Cartesian coordinates, set radius to zero and give X and Y 
# origin coordinate functions. 
# (b) For polar coordinates, set the X/Y polar origin values and 
# radius and/or angle functions. 

